<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Cooch Behar State</h1><p class="st-meta">1586 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Cooch Behar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The western successor of the Koch kingdom of northern Bengal, a princely state renowned for its cultured Maharajas until it joined India in 1949.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Koch kingdom rose under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biswa-singha">Biswa Singha</a> in the early 16th century and reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara-narayan">Nara Narayan</a>. After a division of the realm in the 1580s, the western half, Koch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, continued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> Narayan's line, while the eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/koch-hajo">Koch Hajo</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a> ruled the plains between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teesta-river">Teesta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sankosh-river">Sankosh</a> rivers, at times in conflict with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zhabdrung-bhutan">Bhutan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>. A 1773 treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> against Bhutanese incursions made it a tributary state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nripendra-narayan">Nripendra Narayan</a>, married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suniti-devi">Suniti Devi</a>, daughter of the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keshab-chandra-sen">Keshab Chandra Sen</a>, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cooch-behar-palace">Cooch Behar Palace</a> and modernised the administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagaddipendra-narayan">Jagaddipendra Narayan</a> signed a merger agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in August 1949, and Cooch Behar became a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> in January 1950. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gayatri-devi">Gayatri Devi</a>, later Maharani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, was born into its royal family.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cooch-behar-state.jpg" alt="Cooch Behar State" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: LG02 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Cooch_Bihar_State.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1586 CE – 1949 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cooch Behar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nara Narayan, Nripendra Narayan, Jagaddipendra Narayan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Rajbanshi, Bengali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Koch Bihar, Cooch Behar</dd></dl></aside></div>
